---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import EditorialSources from "../../components/EditorialSources.astro";
import { editorialSources } from "../../data/editorialSources";

const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why Seasonal Songs Return Every Year | How Much Music"
  description="What recurring holiday demand can show about a song's public life, and why it does not reveal a private royalty total or recipient."
  type="article"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Seasonal Demand</p>
      <h1>Why seasonal songs return every year</h1>
      <p class="page-updated">Published September 2, 2026</p>
      <p>
        A seasonal song can regain attention because a predictable cultural moment makes it useful
        again. That recurrence is visible in some public chart and certification records, but it is
        not a public royalty statement.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-panel" aria-label="The key distinction">
      <p class="eyebrow">Key Distinction</p>
      <strong>Demand</strong>
      <span>can be public; the eventual recipient and amount usually are not</span>
      <a class="home-action-link" href="#public-record">Read the public record</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="page-jump-list" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#return">Why songs return</a>
    <a href="#public-record">What the record shows</a>
    <a href="#rights">Why rights still matter</a>
    <a href="#limits">Limits</a>
  </nav>

  <section id="return" aria-labelledby="return-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Recurring Use</p>
    <h2 id="return-title">A calendar moment can make a catalog recording useful again</h2>
    <div class="editorial-grid seasonal-driver-grid">
      <article class="card seasonal-driver-card">
        <div class="artist-card-body">
          <p class="eyebrow">1. Recognition</p>
          <h3>The song is easy to find</h3>
          <p>Familiar titles, playlists, radio programming, and social sharing can make a recording easy to rediscover at the relevant time of year.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="card seasonal-driver-card">
        <div class="artist-card-body">
          <p class="eyebrow">2. Repeatable Moment</p>
          <h3>The context returns</h3>
          <p>A holiday, annual event, or other recurring occasion can renew the reason listeners, programmers, and editors reach for a song.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="card seasonal-driver-card">
        <div class="artist-card-body">
          <p class="eyebrow">3. Multiple Uses</p>
          <h3>The song can travel</h3>
          <p>Streaming is one route. Broadcast, retail, user-made videos, and audiovisual use may create other forms of public visibility or licensed activity.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="public-record" aria-labelledby="public-record-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evidence Boundary</p>
    <h2 id="public-record-title">What public seasonal-demand records can show</h2>
    <p>
      Chart history and certification announcements can document repeated public attention. They can
      support a narrow conclusion that a recording has remained culturally and commercially visible.
    </p>
    <ul class="meta-list">
      <li>Recurring chart activity can support discussion of public demand during a particular period.</li>
      <li>Certification context can support discussion of documented industry milestones where the certifying body publishes them.</li>
      <li>Public platform or retailer placement can support identity and availability context, not an undisclosed revenue amount.</li>
      <li>None of these records identifies the amount paid to any artist, writer, label, publisher, estate, or catalog buyer.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="rights" aria-labelledby="rights-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Rights Context</p>
    <h2 id="rights-title">Why recurring attention is not the same as artist income</h2>
    <p>
      A public resurgence can create activity around both the sound recording and the underlying
      musical work. Those are separate copyrighted works, and their ownership and administration can
      differ. The performer associated with the recording may not control every relevant interest.
    </p>
    <p>
      Catalog transactions, publishing agreements, label arrangements, and private recoupment terms
      can further change who receives value. That is why seasonal popularity is useful evidence of
      demand, while a personal-income claim needs records that public charts and certifications do not provide.
    </p>
    <div class="related-link-row">
      <a href={`${baseUrl}insights/music-rights-map/`}>Open the Music Rights Map</a>
      <a href={`${baseUrl}insights/catalog-ownership/`}>Read catalog ownership context</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" class="editorial-basis-card" aria-labelledby="seasonal-limits-title">
    <div class="editorial-basis-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Limits</p>
      <h2 id="seasonal-limits-title">How to read seasonal-song claims carefully</h2>
      <p>
        A song's annual visibility can be meaningful without proving a fixed annual payment. Use
        public demand evidence to understand why a recording remains active, then look separately at
        the ownership and rights context before drawing conclusions about who benefits.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="related-link-row">
      <a href={`${baseUrl}insights/evergreen-songs/`}>Read the evergreen-song guide</a>
      <a href={`${baseUrl}case-studies/`}>Browse source-backed case studies</a>
      <a href={`${baseUrl}contact/`}>Suggest a public source</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <EditorialSources sources={editorialSources.seasonal} title="Seasonal demand and rights background" />
</BaseLayout>
